\details{
Compare Two Mitochondrial Genome Sequences
\subsection{Alignment}{

The two genome sequences are aligned with \code{\link[pwalign:pairwiseAlignment]{pwalign::pairwiseAlignment()}}
using a global Needleman-Wunsch alignment with a +1/−1 nucleotide
substitution matrix, gap-open penalty 10, and gap-extension penalty 0.5.
Non-ACGT characters are replaced with N before alignment to avoid IUPAC
ambiguity-code lookup errors.  Consecutive gap columns of the same type are
merged into a single indel event.
}

\subsection{Annotation}{

Genomic features (CDS, rRNA, tRNA) are obtained by downloading the GenBank
flat-file record for \code{seq1} via \code{\link[rentrez:entrez_fetch]{rentrez::entrez_fetch()}}.  If the download
fails (e.g. \code{seq1} is a raw sequence without an accession), the function
falls back to \code{\link[=fetch_annotations]{fetch_annotations()}} for CDS-only information; rRNA columns
are then set to \code{NA}.
}

\subsection{Protein effect}{

For SNPs inside annotated CDS features the reference and alternate codons are
extracted from the reference sequence and classified as \code{"synonymous"},
\code{"missense"}, or \code{"nonsense"}.  For indels inside CDS features, the effect
is \code{"frameshift"} when \code{length(indel) \%\% 3 != 0}, else \code{"inframe_indel"}.
}

\subsection{rRNA structure}{

When \code{fold_rrna = TRUE} and ViennaRNA \code{RNAfold} is on the PATH, the entire
rRNA sequence (12S or 16S, separately) is extracted from each genome using
the annotated coordinates and folded with \code{\link[=fold_rna]{fold_rna()}}.  All mutations
falling in the same rRNA are annotated with the same delta values
(alt − ref).  If \code{fold_rrna = FALSE} or \code{RNAfold} is not found, a warning
is issued and rRNA columns are set to \code{NA}.
}

\subsection{MDP ORFs}{

When \code{scan_mdps = TRUE}, \code{\link[=scan_orfs]{scan_orfs()}} is called on both genomes
(\code{both_strands = TRUE}, \code{circular = TRUE}) with \code{min_orf_length = min_mdp_nt}.
ORFs that overlap an annotated CDS by more than 50 \% of the ORF length are
excluded.  For each mutation, the ORFs overlapping that position in the
reference and query are compared: ORFs present only in the reference are
"lost"; those present only in the query are "gained"; those present in both
but with different protein sequences are "changed".
}
